course: biology
lesson: 100
title: Sea lions, seals and otters
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: A sea lion claps its flippers and walks up the rocks on all four, while a harbour seal next door wriggles on its belly and floats upright asleep like a bottle in the water.
explain: The quickest way to tell them apart is to count and look: a sea lion has small ear flaps and can turn its back flippers forward, a true seal has only ear holes and cannot.
explain: Their senses are tuned for the sea: a seal's whiskers feel the swirling trail left by a fish, so it can follow a fish it cannot even see.
explain: Sea otters float on their backs, crack shells on stones and hold paws while they sleep, and by eating sea urchins they keep whole kelp forests alive.
explain: A seal pup alone on a beach is usually not lost: its mother may be fishing nearby, so the right thing is to keep away and call a rescue group.
fact: Sea lions can walk on all four flippers by turning their back flippers forward and have small ear flaps; true seals, like the harbour seal, have no ear flaps and wriggle on land. Sea lions often squabble loudly over the best resting spots on the rocks.
fact: Trainers use a whistle to say «yes, that's right» at the exact moment, then give a fish, so sea lions learn to open their mouths and lie still for vet checks without sleep medicine.
fact: Many zoos have stopped tricks like ball balancing and show natural behaviour instead.
fact: Seals can sleep floating upright with their noses up, hold their breath for many minutes and close their nostrils tightly when they dive.
fact: A seal's whiskers feel tiny movements in the water, and in experiments a blindfolded seal followed the trail of a fish model seconds after it passed.
fact: Sea otters hold paws or wrap themselves in kelp while sleeping so they do not drift apart, and they have the thickest fur of any animal instead of a fat layer.
fact: Sea otters smash shellfish on a stone laid on the chest and keep a favourite stone in a loose skin pocket under the arm.
fact: Sea otters eat sea urchins that eat kelp; when otters were hunted almost to extinction for fur, urchins spread and kelp forests vanished in places.
fact: An animal that keeps a whole habitat in balance, like the sea otter, is called a keystone species.
fact: Asian small-clawed otters, the smallest otters in the world, juggle pebbles with their paws, perhaps as play or practice for handling food.
fact: Otters keep in touch with squeaks, chirps and whistles, and they came back to many British rivers after harmful farm chemicals, which had poisoned otters and the fish they ate, were banned.
fact: The walrus is a relative of seals and sea lions; it uses its two long tusks to haul itself onto the ice and to show strength, and finds clams with its whiskers; walruses are huge, need cold seas and eat lots of clams, so zoos hardly ever keep them.
fact: Some zoos and aquariums rescue seal pups that are ill or have lost their mothers, feed them until they are strong and release them into the sea.
exercise: How can a sea lion walk on all four flippers? | It turns its back flippers forward | It has four real legs, just like a dog | It uses its tail as a fifth leg | It cannot, it only wriggles
exercise: Which has small ear flaps you can see? | The sea lion | The harbour seal | The big walrus | Both seals and walruses
exercise: How does a true seal move on land? | It wriggles on its belly | It walks on four flippers | It hops along on its tail | It rolls sideways like a log
exercise: Why does the trainer blow a whistle at the exact moment? | To say "yes, that's right" before the fish comes | To scare the sea lion so it obeys the trainer | To call other sea lions to watch | To tell the crowd to clap
exercise: Why are sea lions trained to open their mouths? | So vets can check their teeth without sleep medicine | So they can catch the balls thrown to them in the show | So they look scary to visitors | So they can sing in the show
exercise: How can a harbour seal sleep in the water? | Floating upright with its nose up | Lying flat on the bottom with its eyes open | Holding onto the keeper | It never sleeps in water
exercise: What does a seal do with its nostrils when it dives? | Closes them tightly | Opens them wide to sniff | Fills them with sand first | Breathes water through them
exercise: What do a seal's whiskers feel? | The swirling trail left by a swimming fish | The warmth of the sun on the water | The taste of the water | The sound of boats
exercise: Why do sea otters hold paws while sleeping? | So they do not drift apart | To keep each other's paws warm | To stop each other diving | To show who is the boss
exercise: What keeps a sea otter warm instead of a fat layer? | The thickest fur of any animal | A hard shell on its back like a turtle | A layer of seaweed | Its hot breath
exercise: How does a sea otter open a hard shell? | It smashes it on a stone on its chest | It bites it open with its sharp tusks | It drops it from a cliff | It waits for the shell to open
exercise: Where does a sea otter keep its favourite stone? | In a loose skin pocket under its arm | Stuffed in its cheek like a hamster | Buried on the sea floor | Under its tail
exercise: What happened when sea otters were hunted almost to extinction? | Urchins spread and kelp forests vanished in places | The kelp forests grew bigger and thicker than ever before | The sea urchins died out too | Nothing changed in the sea
exercise: What is a keystone species? | An animal that keeps a whole habitat in balance | The biggest and strongest animal in a habitat | An animal that lives on rocks | An animal that eats only stones
exercise: What do Asian small-clawed otters do with pebbles? | Juggle them with their paws | Eat them for salt | Build dams with them | Throw them at the keepers for fun
exercise: Why did otters come back to many British rivers? | Harmful farm chemicals were banned | People stopped fishing in the rivers | Otters were brought from Australia | The rivers became much warmer in winter
exercise: What does a walrus use its tusks for? | Hauling itself onto the ice and showing strength | Digging up clams to eat | Spearing fish | Cutting through the thick ice to make breathing holes
exercise: Is a walrus a kind of seal? | It is a relative of seals and sea lions | Yes, it is a baby elephant seal | No, it is a kind of whale | No, it is a close relative of elephants
test: 3 walruses on the ice, no sorry 5. how many tusks | 10 | 6 | 5 | 20
test: The show has 2 sea lions and 1 harbour seal. How many ear flaps can you see? | 4 | 6 | 2 | 0
test: In Finding Dory, the sea lions push a friend off their rock and bark "Off! Off!". Which part is true to life? | Real sea lions squabble loudly over the best resting spots | Real sea lions speak English words | Real sea lions live alone, each on its own single rock | Real sea lions never make any sound
test: kid on the beach found a seal pup on its own, wants to carry it home. what do u tell him | Keep away and call a rescue group; mum may be near | Pick it up gently and carry it down to the sea yourself | Give it some milk from the shop | Take a selfie up close with it
test: A seal is blindfolded in a pool and still catches a fish. How? | Its whiskers follow the swirl the fish left behind | It smells the fish from far away through the water | It hears the fish breathing | It sees through the blindfold
test: Why does a whistle work faster than giving the fish straight away? | It marks the exact moment the animal got it right | It is much louder than the crowd, so the animal can hear it | The fish would sink too fast | Sea lions hate the taste of fish
test: If all the sea otters vanished from a coast, what would happen to the kelp forest? | Urchins would multiply and could eat it bare | The kelp would grow faster than ever, with no otters to eat it | The kelp would turn into coral | Nothing, otters eat only kelp
test: Your friend says sea otters are just big cuddly ducks of the sea, they do nothing much. What is the truth? | They protect whole kelp forests by eating urchins | They eat the kelp forests down to bare rock | They only sleep and never eat | They are birds that cannot fly
test: Which animals have we met that use tools, like the sea otter's stone? | Chimpanzees with sticks and capuchins with stones | Sharks and jellyfish | Flamingos with feathers and penguins with pebbles | Goats and sheep
test: Why are walruses hardly ever kept in zoos? | They are huge, need cold seas and lots of clams | They are far too shy to be seen by visitors | They are too small to find in a tank | They eat only penguins
test: A big otter-like animal in a zoo pool clearly has ear flaps and walks on four flippers. What is it? | A sea lion | A harbour seal | A young walrus | A sea otter
test: How could cleaner rivers bring back otters? | More fish survive, and otters are no longer poisoned | Clean water is warmer for otters | Otters only like the taste of clean, fresh water | Clean rivers flow faster for swimming
exam: Identify the feature by which a sea lion may most readily be distinguished from a true seal. | The presence of small external ear flaps and the capacity to walk upon four flippers | The possession of two long tusks and a thick coat of fur | The absence of whiskers | The possession of a striped coat
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the ecological role of the sea otter? | By consuming sea urchins it preserves the kelp forests and is thus a keystone species | It consumes the kelp forests in great quantity and thereby destroys them entirely | It has no discernible effect upon its habitat | It fertilises the kelp with its droppings alone
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a solitary seal pup discovered upon a beach. | It ought to be left undisturbed and a rescue organisation notified | It ought to be carried at once into the sea | It ought to be fed with cow's milk | It ought to be removed to a private dwelling
exam: It is given that four walruses repose upon an ice floe whilst one further walrus joins them. Ascertain the aggregate number of tusks. | Ten | Eight | Five | Twelve
